Package: isofill
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Description: fill a raster from rasterised contour lines
 isofill turns a raster of contour lines into a continuous surface. For each
 cell it gathers the contours within a search radius which are in line of sight
 - the straight line to them crossing no other contour - takes the pair with the
 steepest gradient across the cell, and interpolates between them.
 .
 Where no two differing elevations are in sight it declines to fill the cell at
 all, rather than extrapolating from one. That is what keeps water enclosed by a
 coastline empty, and stops a headland casting a plume of invented elevation
 downrange. A second pass interpolates each row and column between its known
 values, anchored at zero beyond both ends, so those declined cells settle at the
 level of whatever surrounds them.
 .
 It exists because the obvious tools do not do this. gdal_fillnodata will
 interpolate from a single sample, which terraces the surface into plateaus and
 pushes elevation out into open water; gdal_grid triangulates, which is far
 slower and takes no account of contours as barriers.
